      "writtenOn": "2019-09-23T18:07:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think I understood the motivation for this - if the buffer will be accessed in android software, a sw usage bit is set at allocation time, due to which gralloc decides to allocate a buffer amenable to SW. If it will not be accessed, why is this hint necessary? Would there be cases where the SW usage flag is never set at allocation but the buffer is still accessed?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 235,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 235,
        "endChar": 32
      },
      "revId": "cc72fe32fc536152ad9fd57641c7b0a395ee80e9",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2eb83e36_bd14897a",
        "filename": "graphics/mapper/4.0/types.hal",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 235,
      "author": {
        "id": 1073546
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-09-23T18:42:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We will never try to access a buffer on the CPU that didn\u0027t have the CPU flags set. \n\nWe added this because we thought that there could be times when a buffer was allocated with the CPU flags set but the format definition was too vague for generic android code to operate on the buffer.\n\nHowever, as I am going through the formats it looks like that may not be the case. Is there ever a time when CPU flags are set but the buffer could be in an unexpected/un-generically-defined way?",
      "parentUuid": "a4bc659c_dda89df7",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 235,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 235,
        "endChar": 32
      },
      "revId": "cc72fe32fc536152ad9fd57641c7b0a395ee80e9",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
